<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Beauvais</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Caesaromagus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Beauvais is a city in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oise">Oise</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> in Hauts-de-France. It grew from Caesaromagus, the Roman chief town of the Bellovaci, and became a bishopric. Its Gothic cathedral of Saint-Pierre has the highest choir vault in the world, which partly collapsed in 1284, and its tapestry manufactory was founded in 1664.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/beauvais.jpg" alt="Beauvais" width="320" height="249" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Diliff · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Beauvais_Cathedral_Exterior_1,_Picardy,_France_-_Diliff.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Beauvais is a town and commune in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, the prefecture of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oise">Oise</a> département in the Hauts-de-France region, 75 kilometres north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. It lies at the foot of wooded hills on the left bank of the Thérain at its confluence with the Avelon. Its ancient ramparts have been destroyed and it is now surrounded by boulevards. The region around it is called the Beauvaisis.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> knew it by the Gallo-Roman name Caesaromagus, the capital of the Belgic tribe of the Bellovaci. Some hold that it was the fortified town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bratuspantium">Bratuspantium</a> that surrendered to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a>, as De Bello Gallico reports, while others identify that town with Vendeuil-Caply or Bailleul sur Thérain. In the ninth century it became a county, which passed in about 1013 to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of Beauvais, who became peers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> from the twelfth century. At royal coronations the Bishop of Beauvais wore the royal mantle and, with the Bishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/langres">Langres</a>, raised the king from his throne to present him to the people.</p><p>The town defended itself against the English in 1346 and was besieged by them again in 1433. The siege by the Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> in 1472 was noted for the heroism of the town's women under Jeanne Hachette, remembered in a procession on 27 June in which women take precedence over men. Beauvais was extensively damaged in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a> and again in June 1940; much of the older part was all but destroyed, and the cathedral was badly damaged before British forces liberated the town on 30 August 1944.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The cathedral of Saint-Pierre, begun in 1247 under Bishop William of Grès, was raised an extra 5 metres to make it the tallest cathedral in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and the vaulting of the interior exceeds 46 metres. Work stopped in 1284 when the vaulting of the choir collapsed, and the fall of a too-ambitious central tower in 1573 halted it again. The building consists only of a transept and choir with an apse and seven apse chapels; a small Romanesque church of the 10th century, the Basse Oeuvre, occupies the site meant for the nave. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stained-glass">stained glass</a> of the 13th, 14th and 16th centuries includes work by Engrand Le Prince.</p><p>The state tapestry manufactory dates from 1664. The church of Saint-Étienne is a Romanesque and Gothic building of the early 12th to late 16th centuries, and a former episcopal palace of the 16th century houses the departmental museum. Beauvais–Tillé Airport, dating from the 1930s, serves as a gateway to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Beauvais” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Bellovaci lived in the modern Oise and Picardy around <b>Beauvais</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/bellovaci">Bellovaci</a></li></ul></section>
